rently have **two pair**: Queens and Kings (using your Q♥ and the Q♣ and K♣ on the board). This is a strong hand.  \n- Additionally, there are **three clubs** on the board, meaning a flush is possible. You do not have a club, so you cannot make the flush. However, if your opponent holds any two clubs (especially high ones like A♣ or J♣), they could have a flush.  \n- There is also a possibility that your opponent has a higher two pair (e.g., with K-Q, K-K, or even A-K), but given the lack of aggression in Round 0 (both players checked), it’s unlikely they have a very strong hand like trips or a flush.  \n- Since both players checked on the pre-flop (Round 0), this suggests neither had a super strong starting hand. Your two pair is likely ahead of many hands in this context.  \n- Checking now (action 0) keeps the pot small and may induce a bluff from your opponent later, but risks giving them a free card to catch up (e.g., complete a flush).  \n- A small raise (100) builds the pot and protects your hand slightly by pricing out weak draws. Given the flush draw on the board, charging for cards is prudent.  \n- Going all-in (-2) would be overly aggressive here—it might scare off worse hands and only get called by better hands (like a completed flush or full house), which we don’t want unless we're certain we're ahead.  \n\nReason:  \nRaising to 100 balances value extraction and protection. It grows the pot with a strong hand while discouraging opponents from chasing flush draws for cheap. Given that you’re in the lead and playing against an opponent who showed passivity pre-flop and on the flop, a moderate bet maximizes expected value without overcommitting.  \n\nAnswer: 100"}
