alid action**, that means **there is no pending bet** — so opponent did **not** make a bet that needs calling.\n\nWait — that can’t be.\n\nAlternatively: perhaps both players acted, and now it\'s a new decision point where the last bet was matched.\n\nBut according to history:\n- You raised by 100.\n- Opponent raised by 200.\n\nSo the last action is opponent raising, so you must respond.\n\nBut why is 0 (check) an option?\n\nPossibility: the system uses "0" for call when facing a bet? But usually 0 is check/call.\n\nLooking at valid actions: [-2, -1, 0, 400, ...]\n\nAnd 400 is the minimum raise — which is twice the previous raise (200 × 2 = 400).\n\nSo logic:\n- You raised by 100.\n- Opponent raised by 200 → so the last raise was 200.\n- To raise now, you must raise by at least 400.\n- To call, you must match the opponent’s total bet contribution this round.\n\nBut **0 is listed as a valid action** — which suggests that "0" here means **call** the current bet.\n\nIn many systems, 0 represents call/check depending on context.\n\nGiven that, likely:\n- 0 = call the 200 bet (since opponent raised by 200, you must pay 200 to stay in).\n- -1 = fold\n- -2 = all-in\n- 400+ = raise (minimum raise amount is 400, because twice the last raise of 200)\n\nSo you’re facing a **call of 200 chips**.\n\nNow, evaluate:\n\n- You have **Q♥8♦**\n- Board: **K♣, Q♣, 2♣**\n\nSo your hand: **Pair of Queens**, kicker 8.\n\nBoard has **three clubs** — flush draw possible.\n\nOpponent re-raised your flop bet — very aggressive. Likely has:\n- King (top pair, better than your kicker),\n- Or Q♣ with better kicker,\n- Or a flush draw (like A♣J♣),\n- Or even K♣Q♣ (two pair).\n\nYour hand is vulnerable. Even if you’re ahead now (e.g., against Kx), many turn cards hurt you (any club, any king, any queen with better kicker revealed).\n\nCalling 200 to potentially face another big bet on turn is risky.\n\nRaising to 400+ would represent strength — like you have KQ or QQ — but you don’t. Bluff-raising h