r action is needed — but here, since opponent raised by 841, to call you must match 841.\n\nAh — likely, the system uses "0" to represent **call** the outstanding bet. So even though you’re calling 841, the action is labeled "0". Similarly, raises start at 1682 (which is 841*2).\n\nSo:\n- **0** = call 841\n- **1682** = raise to 1682 total (i.e., you add 1682, which includes covering the 841 call and raising another 841)\n- Anything above 1682 up to your chip stack (~19700 - already committed)\n\nBut you only have **19,700 chips**. You\'ve already bet some earlier (you raised 100 pre-flop, and called some flop action). But you still have plenty left.\n\nNow, evaluating:\n- **Calling (0)**: Safest. Keeps you in the hand. You have two pair, which beats many hands without a flush. But if opponent has flush, you’re beat. Still, folding to a flush draw or weaker pair seems premature.\n- **Raising to 1682**: Shows strength. Could get value from worse hands (like weaker two pair, top pair with bad kicker) or flush draws. But likely, if they have the flush, they’ll call or re-raise. This might only get called by better hands or fold out worse — mixed outcome.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill. You have 19,700, so going all-in would be ~19,700. That’s way over 1682. Risky — you could lose a huge amount when beaten by a flush.\n\nAlso consider: the river card hasn’t come. If you raise now, you end the action (no more betting after river if all-in). But if you call, you preserve position and information.\n\nBut: **your hand is vulnerable**. Any club beats you. Any straight (if someone has J-10) is not possible yet (needs 10 on river). But flush is live.\n\nYet, your **two pair (QQ + KK)** with **A-high** is very strong against non-flush hands. Few players call with Kx or Ax without a club.\n\nGiven the opponent raised on the **turn after the flush card hit**, it\'s highly indicative they have the **flush** or are bluffing. But good players often don’t bluff large on flush turns unless polarized.\n\nThus, **calling (0)** is best — you control losses and keep equity. Raising risks inflating the pot when you may already be behind.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong two pair, but the board pairs the flush on the turn, and your opponent’s 841 bet strongly suggests a flush. Raising may only get action from better hands or force folds from worse (few exist). Calling keeps you in the hand without overcommitting. Going all-in risks your chip lead unnecessarily. Folding gives up too much equity — many missed draws or top-pair hands could be behind. Thus, calling is optimal.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
