lue is the straight draw.\n\nNow, betting context:\n- All players checked through pre-flop, flop, and turn.\n- This is **the turn round** — last betting before river.\n- Opponent checked. Now it's your turn.\n\nSince everyone checked to you, and you’re second to act on this street, and opponent checked, you have option to check or bet.\n\nBut note: game history says:\n- Round 0 (pre-flop): opp 0, you 0 → both checked\n- Round 1 (flop): opp 0, you 0 → both checked\n- Round 2 (turn): opp 0 → opponent checked\n\nSo now it’s your turn on the turn betting round.\n\nYou have position.\n\nYou have a **weak made hand (pair of 5s)** with **eight clean outs to a straight** (2 or 3 on river).\n\nHowever, since the opponent has checked twice, they may have a weak hand or are slow-playing.\n\nBut board has Ace — many players play Ace-rag hands, so opponent could have top pair weak kicker.\n\nYour pair of 5s is likely behind any pair of Aces, 8s, or 10s.\n\nBut if they have nothing, your pair might be best.\n\nBest strategy: **bet for value or as a semi-bluff**?\n\nBut you cannot raise too much because:\n- You want to control pot size\n- Or you can **check to keep opponent in with weaker hands**\n\nAlternatively, a **small bet** could extract value from worse pairs or draw them off.\n\nBut opponent has shown passivity.\n\nIf you bet, you might get called by worse pairs (like 4x, 3x, 2x, maybe 7x), or draws.\n\nBut you also risk folding out worse hands that you beat.\n\nGiven that the pot is likely small (all checks so far), and you have a draw to the nuts, **checking is better** to see the river for free.\n\nWhy bet?\n- If you think you're ahead, bet for value.\n- But with 5s, you're likely not ahead. Board has A, 10, 8 — many overcards.\n\nAlternatively, a **bluff or semi-bluff** could work, but you have no flush draw, only straight draw with 8 outs (~17% chance to hit).\n\nBut betting here might not build enough fold equity.\n\nMoreover, if opponent has a piece (like Ax, 8x, 10